[
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Shall the membership of the Boulder County Board of Commissioners be increased from a three member board to a five member board?",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Who put the measure on the ballot?",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "No position. Boulder County Measure 200 was placed on the ballot through a citizen initiative backed by a group of more centrist Boulder County called “",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Big Tent Boulder",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://www.bigtentboco.org/"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": ".”",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Short Description:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Expands the Boulder County Board of County Commissioners from three members to five. Organizers submitted 18,422 signatures; the Clerk issued a statement of sufficiency on August 5, and the commissioners certified the measure on August 25.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Analysis and Commentary:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "I ultimately decided to go with “No position” on Measure 200, which is a first in the 10+ year history of Boulder Colorado Voter Guide. I have struggled with deciding my vote for many months, and tried to capture all of my thinking here. First I’ll address what I think are the most compelling reasons I found to vote No, and several compelling reasons for a Yes vote.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Compelling reasons to vote No:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Boulder County’s commissioners can move more quickly with only three commissioners. The roles have traditionally been less a legislative body and more managerial. Traditionally, commissioners had a staff and management component. Only since 2020 has the county had a county administrator (similar to a city manager) that staff reported to (and that ",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 8,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "person has recently resigned",
                "type": "extended-text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "type": "link",
            "version": 1,
            "rel": null,
            "target": null,
            "title": null,
            "url": "https://www.longmontleader.com/local-news/boulder-county-administrator-jana-petersen-resigns-to-take-county-executive-role-in-california-12318062"
          },
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": ").",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "While many proponents state that having five commissioners as increasing accountability, I’m not sure how that argument makes sense. By nature, increasing the number of people making a decision would decrease the amount of accountability any one person has in the decision. Regardless of whether we move to a district model (which I oppose) or a three-district, two at-large model, I don’t see how you can argue it makes the system more accountable.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 2
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Moving to a five-district model would be a truly bad outcome. While the current model of three commissioners, all at-large, also has some negative aspects, having five districts would divide up the county awkwardly (cutting up three population areas into five parts) and removes the incentive to work together as a whole to solve the county’s challenges, and promotes the incentive to do what’s best for one’s own district. I write more about this on Boulder County Measure 201.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 3
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "There’s also a cost to increasing the number of commissioners. While not my favorite argument, the reality is that adding two commissioners will increase the county budget by $400-500k, while at the same time Boulder County’s 2027 budget already has significant cuts due to decreased revenues, ",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 8,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "eliminating 36 positions which includes 10-15 layoffs",
                "type": "extended-text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "type": "link",
            "version": 1,
            "rel": null,
            "target": null,
            "title": null,
            "url": "https://bouldercounty.gov/news/on-track-to-fix-structural-budget-deficit/"
          },
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": ". The increased cost might be justified if convincingly showed a tangible improvement in governance but I’m not sure that it will.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 4
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "I’ve spoken with two people I respect greatly, former County Commissioners Will Toor and Elise Jones, who oppose the change, and outlined eight solid arguments against the change as well: ",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 8,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Five current and former Boulder County commissioners: Don’t expand the commission to five seats",
                "type": "extended-text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "type": "link",
            "version": 1,
            "rel": null,
            "target": null,
            "title": null,
            "url": "https://boulderreportinglab.org/2026/02/15/five-current-and-former-boulder-county-commissioners-dont-expand-the-commission-to-five-seats/"
          },
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": " — Boulder Reporting Lab",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 5
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "I don’t particularly trust the group ",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 8,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Big Tent BoCo",
                "type": "extended-text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "type": "link",
            "version": 1,
            "rel": null,
            "target": null,
            "title": null,
            "url": "https://www.bigtentboco.org/about"
          },
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": ", which put the measure on the ballot. The group leans moderate to conservative as a whole, and I presume that the intention is to try to expand their influence in Boulder County. The group has accepted $20,000 from Boulder conservative / Trump voter Dan Caruso, as well as $10,000 from Aquiles La Grave, and $10,000 from Kent Thiry, one of the primary funders behind the narrowly-defeated 2024 ballot measure ",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "detail": 0,
                "format": 8,
                "mode": "normal",
                "style": "",
                "text": "Proposition 131",
                "type": "extended-text",
                "version": 1
              }
            ],
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "type": "link",
            "version": 1,
            "rel": null,
            "target": null,
            "title": null,
            "url": "https://bouldercoloradovoterguide.com/election-guide-2024/"
          },
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": " which would have drastically changed Colorado’s primary and general election system. I like to judge measures based on their merits but like most everything on Colorado’s 2026 ballot, you also have to consider the source.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 6
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "list",
    "version": 1,
    "listType": "bullet",
    "start": 1,
    "tag": "ul"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Now, the Yes arguments that I find compelling:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "How we elect county commissioners has often been not well-contested in the past fifteen years. Because Boulder is a heavily Democratic county, the only potential competitive races are in the Democratic Primary. We had one closely contested race in 2020, one in 2022, and a fairly uneven race in 2012. I do think more races would be contested and open if we moved to five commissioners, but likely more so with the 3-district / 2 at large rather than the five district system.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "The current commissioners don’t seem to discuss policy and work together well due to open meetings laws and requirements for transparency. A discussion between two commissioners about a policy item is problematic when that is also a quorum of the board. It’s possible that having five commissioners might improve communication.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 2
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Right now, a commission of three leads to a lot of bad incentives. I was having a conversation with my fellow Boulder Progressives board member Adrian Fine — Adrian was previously the Mayor of Palo Alto before moving to Boulder. He has a few insights from his time as an elected official that I’d like to share:",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 3
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "detail": 0,
                    "format": 0,
                    "mode": "normal",
                    "style": "",
                    "text": "“With 3: if you're on the losing side, the majority knows that and the dynamics become multiple against singular. With a 4th or 5th vote, the majority cannot just be against 1 person; they have to earn/please/placate at least one more. So the extra vote prevents railroading behavior.”",
                    "type": "extended-text",
                    "version": 1
                  }
                ],
                "direction": "ltr",
                "format": "",
                "indent": 1,
                "type": "listitem",
                "version": 1,
                "value": 1
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "detail": 0,
                    "format": 0,
                    "mode": "normal",
                    "style": "",
                    "text": "If you're trying to assemble a winning coalition, it means it cannot just be you and one other, you are going to have to get a 3rd opinion. Your argument for or against something cannot just be tailored to the one, only other person that matters.",
                    "type": "extended-text",
                    "version": 1
                  }
                ],
                "direction": "ltr",
                "format": "",
                "indent": 1,
                "type": "listitem",
                "version": 1,
                "value": 2
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "detail": 0,
                    "format": 0,
                    "mode": "normal",
                    "style": "",
                    "text": "“And it's not just the elected officials. Staff has the exact same incentive; ‘oh we know the commission is split? let's build X report to persuade the middle’”",
                    "type": "extended-text",
                    "version": 1
                  }
                ],
                "direction": "ltr",
                "format": "",
                "indent": 1,
                "type": "listitem",
                "version": 1,
                "value": 3
              }
            ],
            "direction": "ltr",
            "format": "",
            "indent": 0,
            "type": "list",
            "version": 1,
            "listType": "bullet",
            "start": 1,
            "tag": "ul"
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 4
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Currently, it may be more difficult to meet with the commissioners because there are few of them or work may be held up by the absence of a single commissioner.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 4
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "While not a great argument for a systemic change, a lot of people have significant grievances and mistrust with the current set of commissioners. Some people like the idea of a fresh start with a larger group. However, that’s somewhat moot because two of the commissioners will be term-limited in 2028.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 5
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "list",
    "version": 1,
    "listType": "bullet",
    "start": 1,
    "tag": "ul"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "I tend to be pretty open-minded, and have been grappling with this commissioner question for the past 8+ months. I still don’t have a much better direction than when I first started thinking about the issue. I also find myself thinking about the City of Boulder’s move to directly elect its mayor in 2020 (with the first election in 2023). The measure was popular, I supported it with mixed feelings, and it ",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "passed with 78.14%",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://electionresults.bouldercounty.gov/ElectionResults2020G/"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": " of the vote. I understand the appeal of voting for mayor plus four council people compared to voting for five council people, but I am not sure it necessarily made our governance any better. We do not have a strong mayor system, and decisions are still made by five votes on council and working with the city manager to implement that vision.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "The commissioners ballot measure certainly has trade-offs. I’m not certain that the trade-offs with five commissioners are better than the trade-offs of the current system. We will see what happens.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Additional Reporting, Commentary, and Sources",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
